From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 18:16:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] web-app: LanguageTool spelling & grammar check in the
 composer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The desktop targets ship with Chromium spellcheck off (Electron has no
local hunspell dictionary, see target-electron windows/main.ts), and a
browser grammar add-on can't reach into an Electron/Tauri window. So the
composer gets its own grammar checker: Harper (writewithharper.com,
English only, wasm on this device) or LanguageTool, like the LibreOffice
extension or the Obsidian community plugin, over plain fetch, which works
the same on browser, Electron and Tauri.

Settings -> Experimental -> Composer: a "Grammar & spelling checker"
switch (off by default) and a "Checker" row, shown while off too so the
checker can be picked before anything is sent. Its dialog picks Harper
(the default: nothing leaves the device), LanguageTool cloud
(languagetool.org, free; or Premium with username + API key -- both or
neither, since one alone would quietly fall back to the free API -- which
switches to api.languagetoolplus.com) or LanguageTool self-hosted
(http://host:8081, /v2 and /v2/check suffixes accepted). The server must
sit at the root of its host: the CSPs only allow the /v2/check path, so a
reverse-proxy sub-path (or a query/hash) is rejected in the dialog instead
of failing later as "Failed to fetch"; the check URL is built with new
URL(). A "Check connection" button runs a test sentence and shows the
error otherwise (CORS / --allow-origin being the usual one); only the
latest check may report, so a slow earlier one can't overwrite it. With
no server saved, the address starts out as http://localhost:8081,
languagetool-server's default port, so one on this computer needs no
typing. Picking Harper keeps the LanguageTool settings for switching back.

Harper is loaded only once the check is on with Harper picked: harper.js
is imported at runtime from harper/ next to the app (the web-app serves it
there, like videojs-locales/; it is not bundled), and its ~15 MB wasm only
then. Its WorkerLinter lints in a worker, so typing never waits on it, and
the one linter is shared by every field; a failed load is retried by the
next check. Its lints become the same matches as LanguageTool's (its spans
are UTF-16 offsets already): Spelling/Typo red, style-like kinds blue, the
rest amber; "insert after" suggestions become the flagged text plus the
insertion, so the card's replace path stays one. The dialog has no
"Check connection" for Harper (that would load it while it is off); the
Connectivity view's test check covers it once it is on. Outside the
web-app (Electron, Tauri) harper/ isn't served, so picking Harper there
shows the failed-check icon.

Composer: 1s after typing stops the draft is checked; for LanguageTool
it is POSTed form-encoded to /v2/check with language=auto (a CORS simple
request, so a self-hosted server needs no OPTIONS handling); the previous request is aborted on the
next edit. Issues are underlined (red: misspelling, blue: style, amber:
everything else, as in LanguageTool's own UI) in a transparent,
click-through copy of the text laid over the textarea -- a textarea can't
style parts of its own text and the CSS Highlight API can't reach inside
one. The copy mirrors the textarea's box
and font metrics and its scrollTop, re-synced (imperatively, no React
state) after every render and on resize: the box also moves without
resizing when something above the field changes height, e.g. the "enter a
name" error line above the group description.
Between checks, results are remapped onto the edited text by a common
prefix/suffix diff: matches before the edit keep their offset, matches
after it shift, and a match is dropped once the edit reaches it or the
character next to it (remapMatches, covered by languageTool.test.ts).
Clicking/tapping or arrowing into an underline opens a card anchored to
that word: above it (below when that would leave the window), its pointer
on the word, an accent bar in the issue's colour. It shows LanguageTool's
category name (in the text's language, e.g. "Possible Typo"), the message,
up to five replacements as chips (the first, likely one filled), a quiet
Ignore (per session) and a close button. Card and overlay share a
containing block, so the overlay span's offsets place the card directly.
Typing closes it, so it never pops up mid-sentence. It doesn't own any
keys, so Enter still sends; Tab reaches its buttons. Of overlapping
matches only the first is underlined, and the card only ever opens for an
underlined one. A replacement goes through execCommand('insertText') --
undoable with Ctrl+Z, and it fires `input` so the field's own onChange
takes it like typing (setRangeText() plus a synthetic `input` is the
fallback) -- no per-field plumbing. The overlay reserves 4px
below the box (height and bottom padding alike, so the scroll range is
unchanged) for the last line's wavy underline, which overshoots a tight
line-height: normal line box.

While the checker is on, the field opts out of the browser's spellcheck
(spellcheck=false) and of the LanguageTool browser add-on
(data-lt-active=false), so there is one set of squiggles. While it is off
nothing changes: the composer keeps spellcheck=true, the other fields keep
the browser default, and an installed add-on works as before.

Because of that opt-out a failing check must not be silent: while the
last check failed, a small red filled error icon (Material's, inline:
../Icon only has outlined ones) sits in the field's top right corner,
clear of the resize grip (placed after every render, like the overlay),
its tooltip and
label naming the error; clicking it opens the server dialog. It goes away
with the next check that works. Being in the field, it shows inside the
bio and group dialogs too, where an app toast would sit behind the modal.
The Connectivity view
gets a grammar-checker line while the check is on, under the browser
edition's bridge line: the server, a "Change…" button opening the server
dialog (as the bridge line's opens its own), and a live test check's
result ("working" or "not working: <error>"), which turns back to
"checking…" while a changed server is being tried.

The same check runs in the profile bio (EditProfileDialog), the group
description (EditGroupNameDialog) and the new-group description
(CreateChat): DeltaTextarea takes an opt-in `languageTool` prop rather
than checking every DeltaTextarea, because the proxy dialog uses one for
proxy URLs, which must not be sent anywhere.

CSP: Electron's main.html and Tauri's connect-src allow
http(s)://*:*/v2/check -- any host/port, pinned to that one path, the
same pattern the web edition uses for /unfurl.

SettingsSelector takes forkAdded like SettingsSwitch, so the server row
gets the sloth badge too; the badge markup (until now copied into
SettingsSwitch and EmojiSetSelector) is one exported ForkBadge.

Styles live in their own composer/_language-tool.scss, so the upstream
_composer.scss is untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
